🚀 Feature
torch.jit._overload should have more safeguarding against misuses like, annotated function doesn't have a pass-only body. Currently, behavior is confusing when it is misused.
Motivation
torch.jit._overload requires a specific use pattern, ie declaring multiple unambiguous schemas annotated with torch.jit._overload and a single implementation that is compatible with previously declared schemas, like this:
@torch.jit._overload
def fn(x: int) -> int:
    pass

@torch.jit._overload
def fn(x: str) -> str:
    pass

def fn(x):
    if isinstance(x, int):
        return x + 3
    else:
        return x

If an unaware user uses like
@torch.jit._overload
def fn(x: int) -> int:
    return x + 3

@torch.jit._overload
def fn(x: str) -> str:
    return x

No error is thrown, instead TorchScript silently matches both schema to the second implementation because of python's default rewrite behavior when declaring a function for the second time.
Pitch
Error should be thrown in this and other mis-use cases.
cc @gmagogsfm
